    "Sources: JEE Main 2026 (NTA) and JEE Advanced 2026 (IIT brochure) texts; the 2027 syllabi were not yet published. Every chapter's Main/Adv flag in the chapter list agrees with these texts (phy-magnetism-matter and phy-semiconductors are Main-only; the Advanced text covers only current-loop magnetic moment, no magnetic materials or bar magnet).",
    "Rolling without slipping is flagged Advanced-only because the Main 2026 Unit 5 text does not name rolling. Main papers still set rolling questions under 'rigid body rotation', so study it for Main too.",
    "Some topics are flagged both because the Advanced text covers them through broader headings, not by name: equilibrium of concurrent forces, banked roads, vertical circle (Newton's laws/energy); motion of centre of mass and impulse (Main via momentum conservation); equipotentials and conductors (electric potential, Gauss); power factor and LCR resonance (LCR with a.c.); speed of sound (Main via resonance tube experiment); motional emf (Faraday's law).",
    "Combined experiment topics hide mixed flags: post office box is Advanced-only and galvanometer/diode/Zener/component identification are Main-only; simple pendulum is energy dissipation in Main but g in Advanced; focal length uses parallax in Main but u-v method in Advanced. Experiments were grouped to stay within 12 topics.",
    "Kinetic theory: Advanced Physics names only ideal gas laws and Cv/Cp of monoatomic/diatomic gases, so pressure, rms speed, degrees of freedom/equipartition and mean free path are Main-only here. Cv/Cp in Advanced implicitly needs equipartition, and rms/average speeds appear in the Advanced Chemistry syllabus.",
    "Placement choices: Main lists impulse and momentum conservation under Laws of Motion (placed in phy-center-of-mass), centre of mass under Rotational Motion (placed in phy-center-of-mass), work done on compressing a gas under Kinetic Theory (placed in phy-thermodynamics), and current loop as a magnetic dipole under Magnetism (placed in phy-magnetic-effects). Advanced 'RC, LR, LC and LCR circuits with d.c. and a.c.' is split across phy-capacitors (RC, d.c.), phy-emi (LR, LC) and phy-alternating-current (a.c.).",
    "Vectors (addition, resolution, products) are not named in either 2026 text, so no vector topic was added to phy-motion-2d; they are needed throughout.",
